{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"easygoing-tremendous-frogmouth","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Symptom\nA `preview` route pass, and a legacy `--mode dry-run` pass, perform REMOTE Jira writes during pending-binding recovery. Both modes are documented and reported as no-write.\n\n## Evidence (verified by reading the spine)\n- `run_differs.py` guards the recovery call with exactly ONE condition, `if not scoped_ids` (where `scoped_ids = ctx.filter_local_ids or ctx.selection_ids`). There is no `persist` / `no_write` / mode condition on it.\n- The client it is handed is the real configured transport: `outbound_diff_client = backend.transport`, and `_load_reconcile_backend()` calls `select_backend(load_config())` unconditionally — there is no dry-run branch that substitutes a no-op transport.\n- The keyed-pending branch of `recover_pending_bindings` then calls `client.add_label(key, \"rebar-id:<local_id>\")` and `client.set_entity_property(key, \"local_id\", <local_id>)`. Those are mutations of the remote issue.\n- For contrast, the sibling invariant-filing gate in the same function DOES compose both axes: `skip_invariant_filing = (not persist) or bool(scoped_ids)`. So the omission on the recovery call is asymmetric with the code beside it.\n\nNet effect: `persist=False` suppresses the local binding-store `save()` (it sits inside `reconcile.py`'s `if persist:` block), so the LOCAL side of recovery is correctly not persisted — but the REMOTE label/entity-property writes still go out. A preview therefore mutates Jira and then throws away the local record of having done so, which is also the least recoverable combination.\n\n## Why this was not fixed in RP-02 S3\nRP-02 S3 adds a NEW recovery operation (interrupted-retirement completion) and gates it on `persist and not scoped_ids`. It deliberately does NOT change the pre-existing create-recovery gate: that is a behaviour change to load-bearing anti-duplicate machinery, outside an extraction-plus-addition slice, and S3 T1 carries an explicit acceptance criterion that the create-recovery gate is pinned unchanged. The real behaviour is pinned by test, and the correction is filed here.\n\n## Judgement needed before fixing\nThis is not a one-line gate addition, because there is a real argument on both sides:\n- Suppressing recovery on a preview keeps the promise that preview never mutates.\n- Recovery exists to prevent duplicate Jira issues after a crash. A preview that skips it is harmless, but an operator who only ever runs preview would never recover a keyed-pending binding.\n\nThe likely correct answer is to suppress the REMOTE writes on a no-write pass while still reporting what recovery WOULD do, matching how the rest of preview behaves. That needs its own plan and its own oracles.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A `preview` pass performs zero remote `add_label` / `set_entity_property` calls from pending-binding recovery.\n- [ ] A legacy `--mode dry-run` pass performs zero such remote calls.\n- [ ] A live pass's recovery behaviour is unchanged, including the keyed-pending no-search rule and the three-miss plus 3600-second keyless conjunction.\n- [ ] The chosen disposition (suppress silently vs. report-what-would-happen) is recorded with its rationale.\n- [ ] A regression test drives a no-write pass with a keyed-pending binding and asserts zero remote identity writes.","id":"7851-e06b-9e85-4c9e","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["rp02","reconciler","recovery","no-write"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Preview and dry-run passes perform remote Jira writes during pending-binding recovery"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787136395029186001,"uuid":"018ee15c-454a-4905-bb59-5270ecbf7bf7"}